#### A GI visit that dismissed half of what I came in to ask, five blood tests I had to go look up myself to understand, and the imaging gauntlet that's about to fill my October calendar.

> *Originally published on Substack. Imported to My Ledger as part of the adrenal journey series.*

Quick recap if you're just joining. [Chapter 1](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-1/) covered seventeen years of "unrelated" diagnoses that turned out to share one cause nobody stepped back far enough to see. [Chapter 2](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-2/) covered the team at UT Southwestern that reviewed my case together, and the six-hour episode of elevated blood pressure and heart rate the day after I met my new endocrinologist, as if my own body wanted to underline the point. Here's where the diagnosis stands. A CT on June 23rd found bilateral adrenal adenomas, 2.7 cm on the right and 2.5 cm on the left. Cushing's syndrome was confirmed on August 14th, ACTH-independent. My 24-hour urine cortisol came back at 221 mcg, about 4.4 times the upper limit of normal.

This chapter is about a different kind of appointment. It was with a practice I've been going to for years, for something else entirely, and it ran into the same wall as the first two chapters.

##### The visit

On September 9th, I saw my GI doctor's PA at Texas Digestive Disease Consultants. I've been his colon surveillance patient for a while. That relationship started before any of this, but it could very well be part of the bigger picture. Once Cushing's came into it, I had real GI questions that needed a specialist's judgment, not a Google search. So I did what this whole series keeps coming back to. I did my homework ahead of time so a fifteen-minute visit couldn't shrink me down to fifteen minutes.

I wrote a six-page briefing before I went. Diagnosis up front, my current labs attached, and seven numbered sections of specific questions. Did my iron stores, still low after three months of oral supplements, justify an IV iron infusion? Could too much cortisol, Mounjaro, or both be suppressing my stomach acid enough to explain that and other absorption problems? Was that worth testing? Did my B12, folate, and homocysteine numbers point to a malabsorption problem instead of a diet problem? Does a Cushing's diagnosis that's likely been there for years change anything about how often I need colon polyp checks? Was SIBO worth looking into now, or later? I even left blank lines under each question for the PA to write answers on, so nothing got lost between the appointment and my memory of it.

I'm not printing the whole document here. It's dense, it's clinical, and most of you didn't come here for a deep dive into lab values. But here's how the visit went. The PA moved fast, waved off or redirected a good chunk of what I'd written down, and ordered five tests without explaining why those five and not the others I'd asked about. I left more frustrated than informed. That's exactly what all that prep was supposed to prevent.

To be fair to the PA, I don't think September 9th was one person being careless. It's the same fifteen-minutes-per-patient setup Chapter 1 was about, showing up in a different specialty. A patient walks in with six prepared pages, and the system built around that appointment slot has no way to handle it. That doesn't excuse how it felt in the room. It's just the same pattern this series keeps documenting. If you want the longer explanation of why that setup exists, I wrote about it here: ["Your Doctor Didn't Choose Fifteen Minutes. Their Employer Did."](https://v64otd.com/dispatch/doctor-didnt-choose-fifteen-minutes/) It walks through the prior-authorization numbers, the RVU productivity quotas doctors are held to, and the corporate ownership incentives under all of it.

##### What got ordered, and what I had to go learn on my own

Here's what came out of that visit: a Liver Function Panel, a chronic hepatitis panel, a Celiac antibody profile, a Hemochromatosis gene test, and a C-Reactive Protein test. Nobody told me what any of them check for or why those five, so I looked it up myself.

**Liver Function Panel (LFP).** A group of blood markers, enzymes like ALT and AST plus things like bilirubin and albumin, that show how well the liver is working and whether it's under stress. In my case, it's likely tied to two things. One is an ALT elevation I've noticed that tracks with taking aspirin and goes back to normal when I stop. The other is the FibroScan I have scheduled for October 16th, which measures liver stiffness and fat directly. Getting a baseline liver panel before that scan is a reasonable, standard step.

**Chronic Hepatitis panel.** Blood tests for hepatitis B and C, viral infections that can cause long-term liver inflammation and damage. It's a standard part of ruling causes in or out any time a liver workup is underway, no matter how likely any one cause seems.

**Celiac antibody profile (tTG-IgA, DGP IgG/IgA, total IgA).** Celiac disease is an autoimmune reaction to gluten that damages the lining of the small intestine. That's where much of the absorption of nutrients like iron, B12, and folate occurs. The "total IgA" part matters because some people don't make much IgA at all, which can cause the main antibody test to come back falsely negative. Testing total IgA alongside it is how labs catch that. Given everything in my briefing about unexplained iron and B-vitamin problems, this is one of the most relevant tests they ordered. I just wish somebody had said that out loud instead of leaving me to piece it together afterward.

**Hemochromatosis gene test (HFE).** This one confused me at first. Hemochromatosis is a genetic condition that makes the body absorb and store *too much* iron, the opposite of the deficiency I've been dealing with. My best understanding is that it's a standard inclusion when a liver and iron workup are run together. It's a common enough hereditary cause of liver problems that it gets ruled out as a matter of course, not because anyone thinks it applies to me.

**C-Reactive Protein (CRP).** A general marker of inflammation somewhere in the body. It doesn't point to a cause on its own, but it's a cheap, common way to see whether something inflammatory is going on that more targeted tests should chase down.

What's missing from that list: IV iron or a referral for it, any stomach acid testing, an MMA test to check how my body is actually using B12 instead of just the blood level, RBC folate, a homocysteine retest, any real discussion of the colon polyp question, and any real answer on SIBO. I'm not saying it was medically wrong to skip any of that. I'm not qualified to make that call, and some of it may get addressed once these results are in. I'm pointing it out because I wrote it down ahead of time so it wouldn't get lost, but most of it did anyway.

*A quick note: none of this is medical advice, mine or yours. I'm not diagnosing my own labs in public, and I'm not suggesting anyone manage theirs based on what I write here. I'm explaining what these tests are for in plain language, the way any patient deserves. What they mean for my case is a conversation for my actual doctors, and I'm lucky to have several.*

##### Results pending, and a calendar filling up fast

In the meantime, the next six weeks are filling up fast:

- **September 17th**: a full abdomen scan, which should give a clearer picture beyond what the original adrenal-focused imaging showed.
- **October 16th**: the FibroScan, measuring liver stiffness and fat directly, presumably informed by whatever the liver panel and hepatitis results show between now and then.
- **October 26th**: adrenal vein sampling (AVS), the procedure Chapter 2 ended on. This is the one that decides the path forward: which gland, if either one on its own, is driving the extra cortisol, and whether surgery on one side, medication, or something else is the right next move.

Three procedures, three different specialties, spread over about six weeks. As of today, I'm the only person looking at all three on one calendar.

##### A Google review, and the timing of a voicemail

The day after the appointment, the office sent the usual follow-up: a link to leave a Google review. I left one, and I didn't soften it. I wrote what happened, in about the same terms I've used here. The practice replied fairly quickly and asked me to contact the office directly to discuss it. I haven't, and for now I don't plan to. I'm leaving the review right where it is and watching to see whether it comes up at a future visit. It's a small, unscientific test of whether a written record changes how a system responds, which seems about right for this series.

Then, at almost the same time, I got a voicemail. The PA had spoken with my GI doctor directly, and he now recommended an upper endoscopy (EGD) before my follow-up visit. I'll be honest about where my mind went, because I think it's a fair question, not a paranoid one. Did that come from someone taking a real second look at my chart, or from a critical review landing in an inbox at the same time? I don't know, and I'm not going to pretend I do. Either is possible. I'm noting the timing, not accusing anyone.

##### The same thread, a different room

I said in Chapter 1 that the problem underneath my story isn't unique to me. I meant that broadly, not just about the endocrine system and not just about the doctors who happened to be in the room. Fifteen-minute visits and one-specialty silos don't stop being a problem when the specialty changes. I brought six organized pages built to hold up in a rushed appointment, and they still didn't fully survive it. That's not a story about one PA having a bad day. It's the same story as Chapter 1, with a different name on the door.

Chapter 4 comes when the abdomen scan, the pending labs, the FibroScan, or a callback about that EGD gives me something worth writing about, whichever comes first.
